WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 249: Meriwether Lewis & William Clark: The Corps of Discovery, the Great Unknown, and the Pacific

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.880
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series chronicles the greatest overland exploration in American history.

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Two young captains who led 33 men into 8,000 miles of uncharted wilderness to map a continent.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.880
[Rebecca] Here is George with the epic saga of Meriwether Lewis and William Clark.

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:47.840
[George] In the spring of 1803, President Thomas Jefferson pulled off the greatest real estate transaction in human history, purchasing 828,000 square miles of territory from Napoleonic France for $15 million, doubling the geographic size of the United States overnight.

7
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.360
[George] Yet this colossal Western Empire was a complete terra incognita to the civilized world.

8
00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:01.040
[George] European maps ended abruptly at the Mississippi River, and popular imagination filled the interior with living woolly mammoths.

9
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.1000
[George] Welsh Indians, active volcanoes, and mountains of pure rock salt.

10
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:22.240
[George] Jefferson envisioned an audacious scientific and diplomatic expedition to ascend the Missouri River, cross the Rocky Mountains, and locate the long-sought Northwest Passage, a direct water route to the Pacific Ocean that would link American commerce to the wealthy markets of Asia.

11
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:31.680
[George] To lead this corps of discovery, Jefferson selected his brilliant, 29 year old private secretary, Captain Meriwether Lewis of the 1st U.S.

12
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.640
[George] Infantry.

13
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:41.280
[George] Lewis was a brooding intellectual, gifted naturalist, and seasoned frontiersman, but he understood the immense perils of the journey.

14
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:55.360
[George] Lewis insisted on sharing equal co command with his closest friend and former Army superior, 33-year old William Clark, a red-haired, charismatic frontiersman, master cartographer, and peerless riverman.

15
00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:09.720
[George] In an age of rigid military hierarchy, Lewis and Clark operated as a completely seamless, selfless dual command, their fraternal trust and mutual respect never once faltering across thousands of miles of mortal peril.

16
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:24.040
[George] Jefferson spent months personally tutoring Meriwether Lewis in the White House Library, teaching him celestial navigation, lunar distances, botany, and ethnology to ensure the expedition returned with rigorous scientific data.

17
00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:41.400
[George] On May 14, 1804, the Corps of Discovery, comprising 33 handpicked young soldiers, civilian boatmen, Clark's towering enslaved African American manservant York, and Lewis's massive Newfoundland dog seamen, cast off from Camp Dubois near St.

18
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.880
[George] Louis aboard a 55-foot keelboat and two wooden pirogues.

19
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:54.920
[George] For six grueling months, they battled the furious currents, collapsing sandbanks, and submerged snags of the Missouri River, the big muddy.

20
00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:07.240
[George] Rowing, polling, and hauling the heavy keelboat against the current using tow lines across mudbanks infested with clouds of mosquitoes, ticks and rattlesnakes, the men labored through blistering summer heat.

21
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:17.400
[George] In present-day Iowa, Sergeant Charles Floyd died of a ruptured appendix, becoming the expedition's sole fatality across 28 months of hazardous travel.

22
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:34.040
[George] Lewis, an accomplished amateur botanist, meticulously pressed hundreds of unrecorded plant specimens and described new animals never before seen by science, including the pronghorn antelope, the coyote, the bighorn sheep, and the black-tailed prairie dog.

23
00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.880
[George] Even crating a live prairie dog and shipping it back across the continent to President Jefferson.

24
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:55.600
[George] In late October, having hauled their boats 1600 miles upriver, the expedition arrived in central North Dakota, constructing Fort Mandan among the hospitable villages of the Mandan and Hidatsa nations to endure the bitter, subzero northern winter.

25
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:10.240
[George] The keelboat was an engineering marvel of the frontier, outfitted with swivel blunderbuses, twenty two oars and a canvas sail, capable of carrying twenty tons of trade goods, mathematical instruments, and gunpowder reserves.

26
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:25.600
[George] During the subzero winter at Fort Mandan, where temperatures plunged to 45 degrees below zero, Lewis and Clark hired a French Canadian fur trader named Taosink Charbonneau and his young 16-year-old Lemhai Shoshone wife, Sakagawea.

27
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:36.560
[George] Sakagawea had been captured from her mountain homeland by Hidotsa raiders years earlier and spoke both Shoshone and Hidotsaw, making her an invaluable cultural ambassador.

28
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:49.520
[George] In February 1805, Sakagawea gave birth to a healthy infant son, Jean Baptiste, affectionately nicknamed Pompey by Clark, carrying the baby on her cradleboard for thousands of miles.

29
00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:59.440
[George] In April 1805, the permanent party of 33 departed Fort Mandan in six dugout canoes, pushing westward into completely uncharted country.

30
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:10.160
[George] In June, near modern Great Falls, Montana, Lewis encountered the majestic Great Falls of the Missouri, five thunderous cascading waterfalls dropping over 80 feet.

31
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:16.000
[George] But the sublime beauty concealed a nightmare of physical suffering, to bypass the cataracts.

32
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:29.520
[George] The men spent an entire month portaging their heavy dugout canoes and 18 tons of baggage 18 miles overland across broken limestone terrain carpeted with sharp prickly pear cactus spines that stabbed through their moccasins.

33
00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:38.920
[George] Enduring sudden violent hailstorms and frequent terrifying encounters with colossal grisly bears that absorbed multiple rifle balls before falling.

34
00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:52.680
[George] Sakagaway's keen eye for natural landmarks proved essential as the Missouri River fragmented into countless braided channels, guiding the captains past impassable shoals and through the towering stone ramparts of the White Cliffs.

35
00:05:52.680 --> 00:06:07.160
[George] On August 12, 1805, at Lemhigh Pass along the modern border of Montana and Idaho, Meriwether Lewis achieved a lifelong geographical dream, standing at the ultimate mountain spring source of the mighty Missouri River.

36
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:14.760
[George] He straddled the tiny rivulet with one foot on either bank and crossed the continental divide into the Pacific drainage basin.

37
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] But instead of the gentle downhill river leading directly to the Pacific Ocean that Thomas Jefferson had predicted, Lewis crested the pass and looked westward, beholding a heartbreaking, terrifying vista, endless, jagged ranges of snowcapped granite mountains stretching as far as the human eye could see.

38
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:41.640
[George] Without pack horses to carry their heavy baggage over these impassable mountains before the fast-approaching winter, the expedition faced certain death.

39
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:49.000
[George] Days later, Lewis made cautious contact with a wary band of Lemhai Shoshone horsemen led by Chief Kamowaite.

40
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:56.440
[George] When Sakagawiya was brought into the Council Lodge to translate the negotiations, an astonishing, tearful miracle occurred.

41
00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:05.320
[George] Sakagawiya looked into Kamawaite's face, shrieked with joy, threw her buffalo blanket around him, and broke into uncontrollable weeping.

42
00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.520
[George] The chief was her own long-lost brother, whom she had not seen since her childhood kidnapping.

43
00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:18.480
[George] The overjoyed Shoshone provided the expedition with twenty-nine essential pack horses, pack saddles, and an expert native guide.

44
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:32.400
[George] The emotional reunion between Sakagawea and Kamawate dissolved weeks of suspicion between the two cultures, transforming what could have been a hostile confrontation into a lifelong alliance between the United States and the Shoshone nation.

45
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:43.680
[George] Guided by an elderly Shoshone known as Old Toby, the core of Discovery entered the towering labyrinth of the Bitterroot Mountains along the treacherous Lolo Trail in September 1805.

46
00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.520
[George] It was the most agonizing crucible of the entire expedition.

47
00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:57.440
[George] For eleven freezing days, the men stumbled through knee deep snow drifts along dizzying cliff ledges, horses slipping down rocky ravines and smashing baggage.

48
00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.120
[George] In the dense pine forests, all wild game vanished.

49
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.160
[George] There were no deer, elk, or bears to hunt.

50
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:17.760
[George] Shivering in tattered buckskins without food, reduced to absolute starvation, the men were forced to kill and eat three of their precious young pack colts, boiling portable soup and candle wax tallow to stay alive.

51
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:27.840
[George] Emerging half-dead, emaciated, and delirious from the mountains onto the sunlit white prairie in Idaho, they collapsed among the villages of the Nez Perce Nation.

52
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:40.800
[George] The generous Nez Purse, led by Chief Twisted Hare and an elderly woman named Watcuways who urged peace, fed the starving explorers dried salmon and sweet camas root bulbs, saving their lives.

53
00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:52.720
[George] Regaining their strength, the men used traditional fire methods to hollow out five new dugout canoes, launching into the roaring white water rapids of the Clearwater and Snake Rivers toward the Columbia.

54
00:08:52.720 --> 00:09:05.960
[George] The crossing of the Bitterroots pushed the men to the absolute outer boundaries of human endurance, as frostbite, dysentery and hypothermia claimed horses and supplies on treacherous mountain paths buried under autumn snow.

55
00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:16.760
[George] Hurling down the rapids of the mighty Columbia River through the towering basalt gorges of the Cascade Mountains, the expedition tasted saltwater in early November 1805.

56
00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:30.120
[George] On November 7, as the foggy river widened into a vast, 15 mile wide estuary surrounded by mossy old growth spruce forests, William Clark scribbled his immortal, ecstatic phonetic journal entry.

57
00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:46.200
[George] Ocean in view, oh the joy, nine days later, at Cape Disappointment on the open Pacific coast, the Corps of Discovery stood upon the windwipped sandy beaches watching the colossal, rolling breakers of the Pacific Ocean smash against the headlands.

58
00:09:46.200 --> 00:10:02.040
[George] They had crossed an entire continent on foot and canoe, needing to choose a protected location to construct their winter encampment, Lewis and Clark conducted an extraordinary, unprecedented democratic vote, for the first recorded time in American history.

59
00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:14.120
[George] An enslaved African American man, York, and a Native American woman, Sakagawea, cast equal, recorded ballots alongside military officers to decide where the group would winter.

60
00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:27.880
[George] Constructing Fort Clatsop on the southern banks of the Columbia, the expedition endured four months of relentless, drenching coastal rain, boiling seawater to produce four bushels of salt to cure elk meat for the return journey.

61
00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:41.960
[George] The Democratic vote at Fort Clatsop was a radiant premonition of American democracy, where individual merit and equal human dignity superseded all boundaries of race, gender, and military rank on the shores of the Pacific.

62
00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:58.000
[George] In March 1806, the Corps of Discovery turned their faces homeward, retracing their route across the Bitterroot snowfields, splitting into two exploration parties to map the Marias and Yellowstone rivers, and reuniting safely at the confluence of the Missouri.

63
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:10.000
[George] On September 23, 1806, after 28 months and 8,000 miles of wilderness travel, 33 weathered, buckskinclad explorers rode their canoes into the harbor of St.

64
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:10.800
[George] Louis.

65
00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.360
[George] The entire frontier metropolis turned out on the docks cheering and firing cannons in ecstatic celebration.

66
00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.440
[George] Eastern newspapers had long since given up the expedition for dead.

67
00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.360
[George] Lewis and Clark returned with priceless scientific treasures.

68
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:45.600
[George] Documenting 178 previously unknown plant species, 122 new animal species, establishing diplomatic contacts with fifty Native American sovereign nations, and producing William Clark's legendary map of Western North America, which fundamentally redefined the geography of the globe.

69
00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:56.960
[George] Meriwether Lewis died tragically three years later under mysterious circumstances along the Natchez Trace, while William Clark served for decades as the honored superintendent of Indian Affairs in St.

70
00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:57.680
[George] Louis.

71
00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:11.040
[George] Together, Lewis and Clark ignited the continental imagination of the American Republic, proving that courage, scientific curiosity, and unbreakable fraternal fidelity could conquer the greatest wilderness on Earth.

72
00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:24.960
[George] William Clark's painstakingly hand-drawn master map of the American West, compiled from celestial readings and native accounts, permanently erased the mythical geography of the continent and opened the horizon to a continental nation.

73
00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:35.560
[George] The expedition returned with over 178 unknown plant specimens and 122 animal species, permanently altering world science.

74
00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:51.480
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 250: Sakagaoya, The Shoshone Guide, The Lemhai Reunion, and the Golden Dollar.

75
00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:54.040
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

